.TH CITE 1 "2026-08-28" "cite 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cite \- verify Markdown citations against Context evidence
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cite
.I evidence.jsonl
.br
.B cite version
.br
.B cite help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cite
reads normalized Context JSONL from one regular file, reads a candidate answer
from standard input, and prints the candidate unchanged only after every ctx
reference is validated.
.PP
A citation is the literal Markdown string
.BR [ref](citation.url) .
The ref and URL must be paired in one evidence record. At least one valid
citation is required, and every ctx: occurrence in the candidate must belong to
such a citation.
.PP
Cite checks citation identity only. It does not prove that the record supports
the prose, every claim is cited, or a source is authoritative.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "cite " evidence.jsonl
Validate the candidate on standard input and print it unchanged on success.
.TP
.B cite version
Print the version. The aliases -V and --version are accepted.
.TP
.B cite help
Print a command summary. The aliases -h and --help are accepted.
.SH INPUT
Evidence is normalized context/v1 JSONL. Cite reads ref and citation.url from
each record. Records without a URL may be present but cannot be cited. One ref
paired with two URLs is invalid evidence.
.PP
The candidate is limited to 4 MiB. Evidence is limited to 32 MiB and each
evidence record to 8 MiB. Excess input is rejected, never truncated.
.SH OUTPUT
On success, standard output is byte-for-byte the candidate from standard input.
On rejection or error, standard output is empty. Diagnostics go to standard
error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means the candidate has at least one exact citation and no invalid ctx
occurrence. One means a rejected candidate, empty candidate, no evidence, or no
evidence record with a citation URL. Two means bad usage, malformed or
unreadable evidence, an I/O failure, or input beyond a limit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
context query wikipedia "$q" > evidence.jsonl
ask "Question: $q; answer with exact cited links." < evidence.jsonl |
  cite evidence.jsonl

ply -sh -check 'cite evidence.jsonl >/dev/null' \
  "Question: $q; answer with exact cited links." < evidence.jsonl
.fi
.SH SECURITY
Cite does not open URLs, make network calls, execute programs, or write state.
It is not a Markdown sanitizer, a semantic judge, or proof that a source is
trustworthy. Evidence files and answers may be sensitive.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR context (1),
.BR ask (1),
.BR ply (1),
.BR brief (1),
.BR trail (1)
